games/data/words.json (the Five Letters word lists)

Guess list
  The five-letter words of ENABLE (Enhanced North American Benchmark
  Lexicon), compiled by Mendel Cooper with research by Alan Beale. ENABLE's
  own README states: "The ENABLE master word list, WORD.LST, is formally
  released into the Public Domain. Anyone is free to use it or distribute it
  in any manner they see fit." No licence is needed for this part.

Answer list
  The ENABLE words whose frequency in wordfreq (https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq,
  by Robyn Speer) is at least Zipf 3.0, with plain plurals and past tenses and
  a short blocklist removed. Only the selection of words, made with wordfreq's
  frequency data, is taken from wordfreq; no frequency values are included.
  wordfreq's data files are licensed under the Creative Commons
  Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International licence
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/), and this list, being
  derived from them, is under the same licence.

Credits required by wordfreq's data sources
  wordfreq compiles word frequencies from: Wikipedia; subtitles (OPUS
  OpenSubtitles 2018 and SUBTLEX); news (NewsCrawl 2014 and GlobalVoices);
  books (Google Books Ngrams 2012); web text (OSCAR); Twitter; Reddit; and
  miscellaneous word-frequency lists. In particular:

  - SUBTLEX: the SUBTLEX authors (Marc Brysbaert et al.) must be credited, and
    it must remain clear that SUBTLEX is freely available data.
  - OpenSubtitles: data from OPUS OpenSubtitles 2018, which originates from the
    OpenSubtitles project and may be used with attribution to OpenSubtitles.
  - Google Books Ngrams: acknowledgement of the Google Books Ngram Viewer
    (http://books.google.com/ngrams) as the source.
  - Creative Commons sources: the Leeds Internet Corpus (University of Leeds
    Centre for Translation Studies), Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia, and
    ParaCrawl, a multilingual Web crawl.
  - Twitter data was collected by wordfreq under Twitter's Developer Agreement
    and Policy; none of that text is included here, only word selections.

  Citation: Robyn Speer. (2022). rspeer/wordfreq: v3.0 (v3.0.2). Zenodo.
  https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7199437

